11396959,2025-01-06,Checking or savings account,Checking account,Managing an account,Deposits and withdrawals,"My name is XXXX XXXX XXXX. Approximately three months ago, I started working at a company called XXXX in XXXX, XXXX. After receiving my first two paychecks, I went to Chase Bank to open my very first bank account in the United States. Once my account was opened at the branch, I handed over my two paper checks to the bank representative to deposit them. I was informed that the funds from the checks would be credited to my account the next day. 

The following day, the funds were deposited into my account, but as soon as the funds became available, my account was frozen. Shortly after, I received an email from Chase Bank stating that I needed to contact them. I immediately called the bank to understand the situation and asked why my account had been frozen. 

The bank representative informed me that they were unable to verify the checks I had deposited, and for that reason, they had placed a hold on my account. I asked how this issue could be resolved. The representative advised me to obtain an official phone number from my employer and stated that they would use this number to contact XXXX payroll service center to verify my checks. 

Following their instructions, I contacted my employer and obtained the requested official phone number. I called the bank again and provided them with the number. However, the representative refused to call it. When I asked why, they explained that the number I provided was not listed in a public record, and therefore, they could not use it to verify the checks. 

I went back to my employer and explained the situation. They confirmed that the number was official and provided me with another number. Once again, I contacted the bank and gave them the new number. Unfortunately, they gave me the same response that the number was not listed in a public record and therefore could not be used. 

