Overview of Incident XXXX XXXX ago I settled a transaction, I contacted CitibankXXXX XXXXXXXX to arrange the payment of a debt via credit card as a form of a bill of exchange. During the conversation, I informed the representative that I was recording the exchange for consumer compliance and business purposes. I was then told that Citibank does not consent to being recorded. Despite informing them that I reside in XXXX, a one-party consent state, and that I have the right to record transactions for evidence and compliance purposes, the representative reiterated that recording is against their in-house policy. 

I respectfully expressed my position that this policy is not only improper but also appears to violate state laws, established commercial practices under the Uniform Commercial Code ( UCC ), and my rights as a consumer conducting business. 

Legal Issues Raised 1. State Law on One-Party Consent XXXX is a one-party consent state, which means that under XXXX. Rev. Stat. 542.402 ( 2 ), only one party to a conversation needs to consent to recording for it to be lawful. As I was a participant in the call, I had the legal right to record the exchange without requiring XXXXHome Depots consent . Their refusal to allow me to record conflicts with XXXX law, thereby infringing upon my rights as a resident of the state. 

2. Uniform Commercial Code ( UCC ) Provisions Under UCC Article 3, which governs negotiable instruments and commercial transactions, there is an implied obligation of good faith and fair dealing in all dealings. Specifically : UCC 3-104 : Defines negotiable instruments and sets standards for their use in commercial transactions. Transparency and recordkeeping are critical components of ensuring fair practices. 
UCC 1-201 ( b ) ( 20 ) : Establishes a standard of good faith, defined as honesty in fact and the observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing. By denying the recording of a lawful transaction, Citibank and XXXX XXXX are obstructing the ability to maintain a transparent record, which undermines the principles of good faith and fair dealing. 

Furthermore, refusing to allow a consumer to maintain a record of their transaction creates an imbalance of power, violating the principles of fairness under UCC 2-302, which prohibits unconscionable practices in commercial agreements. 

3. Consumer Protection and Fair Business Practices The refusal to allow recording raises concerns under broader consumer protection principles. The Federal Trade Commission ( FTC ) and CFPB require financial institutions to maintain fair and transparent practices. Internal policies that deny consumers the ability to create their own records of transactions appear to contradict these principles and create an environment of mistrust. 

